<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nea Paphos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as New Paphos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nea Paphos was a harbour city on the south-west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, founded in the late 4th century BCE by Nicocles, the last king of Paphos. It became the capital of Cyprus under the Ptolemies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> places Paul's meeting with the governor Sergius Paulus there. Its Roman villas with floor mosaics are part of a World Heritage Site; the modern town of Paphos grew on the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nea-paphos.jpg" alt="Nea Paphos" width="320" height="117" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Galyonkin from Kyiv, Ukraine · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paphos_panorama_-_Cyprus_-_3_May_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
